('127.112.d.4.F',4,'If the pressure drop across a filter climbs well above its rated value, the filter should be:','["Ignored", "Painted", "Moved", "Changed"]'::jsonb,3,'High drop means loaded.','original'),
('127.112.d.4.G',3,'An accessory that improves indoor air by killing microbes on a coil is a:','["UV light", "Gas valve", "Fuse", "Thermostat"]'::jsonb,0,'UV disinfects surfaces.','original'),
('127.112.d.4.G',4,'An energy recovery ventilator transfers heat and moisture between:','["Water and oil", "Exhaust and incoming fresh air", "Two rooms", "Fuel and air"]'::jsonb,1,'ERVs reclaim energy.','original'),
('127.112.d.4.H',3,'Before cleaning an electronic air cleaner, the technician should:','["Open the fuel line", "Touch the plates live", "Turn off the power", "Add water while on"]'::jsonb,2,'High-voltage cell.','original'),
('127.112.d.4.H',4,'Collector plates of an electronic air cleaner are usually washed with:','["Bleach on live cells", "Gasoline", "Sand", "Mild detergent and water"]'::jsonb,3,'Gentle cleaning.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.A',3,'A common refrigerant leak detector uses a:','["Soap bubble solution", "Hammer", "Ruler", "Glue gun"]'::jsonb,0,'Bubbles show leaks.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.A',4,'An electronic leak detector senses refrigerant by detecting:','["Pressure", "Halogen in the air sample", "Color", "Heat"]'::jsonb,1,'Halogen detectors sniff refrigerant.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.B',3,'Pressurizing with nitrogen and checking with soap bubbles is a:','["Recovery", "Charge check", "Leak test", "Evacuation"]'::jsonb,2,'Standing pressure test.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.B',4,'A pressure that drops steadily on a gauge after charging with nitrogen suggests:','["A full charge", "Cooler gas only", "A good seal", "A leak"]'::jsonb,3,'Falling pressure means leak.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.C',3,'A vacuum pump is used to remove air and moisture from a:','["Refrigerant system", "Duct", "Furnace flue", "Water heater"]'::jsonb,0,'Evacuation.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.C',4,'A micron gauge measures:','["Voltage", "Deep vacuum levels", "Temperature of air", "Gas flow"]'::jsonb,1,'Microns indicate vacuum.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.D',3,'Evacuation boils moisture out of a system by:','["Chilling the pump", "Adding oil", "Lowering pressure so water vaporizes", "Heating the room"]'::jsonb,2,'Low pressure lowers boiling point.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.D',4,'A system is considered dry when the vacuum holds below about:','["760 mm only", "29 inches only", "50,000 microns", "500 microns"]'::jsonb,3,'Deep vacuum target.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.E',3,'A recovery machine pulls refrigerant into a:','["Recovery cylinder", "Fuel can", "Water tank", "Toolbox"]'::jsonb,0,'Recovered refrigerant is stored.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.E',4,'A recovery cylinder must never be filled beyond:','["100 percent", "80 percent of its capacity", "120 percent", "50 liters"]'::jsonb,1,'Leave expansion space.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.F',3,'During recovery, the technician should wear gloves and:','["A tie", "Sandals", "Safety glasses", "A scarf"]'::jsonb,2,'Liquid refrigerant can freeze skin.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.F',4,'Recovery is complete when the system reaches the:','["Same pressure as the shop", "Lowest temperature", "Highest pressure", "Required vacuum level for that refrigerant"]'::jsonb,3,'EPA specifies levels.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.G',3,'A deep vacuum is achieved with a vacuum pump rated for:','["Low micron performance", "High voltage", "High pressure", "Fuel use"]'::jsonb,0,'Pump capability matters.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.G',4,'Using large-diameter hoses for evacuation helps by:','["Slowing it", "Speeding the process", "Cooling the pump", "Adding moisture"]'::jsonb,1,'Bigger hoses flow better.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.H',3,'A charging scale is used to measure the:','["Speed of fan", "Room humidity", "Weight of refrigerant added", "Voltage drop"]'::jsonb,2,'Weigh in the charge.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.H',4,'A manifold gauge set connects to the system''s:','["Air vent", "Thermostat", "Filter", "Service ports"]'::jsonb,3,'Gauges attach at ports.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.I',3,'Purging with dry nitrogen helps remove:','["Moisture and contaminants", "Voltage", "Heat only", "Oil only"]'::jsonb,0,'Nitrogen sweeps the lines.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.I',4,'When brazing, nitrogen flows through the line so that:','["Flux dries", "No scale forms inside the tube", "The tube melts", "The torch cools"]'::jsonb,1,'Prevents oxidation.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.J',3,'The most accurate way to charge many systems is by:','["Guesswork", "Sound", "Weight", "Touch"]'::jsonb,2,'Weighing is precise.','original'),
('127.112.d.5.J',4,'Charging by superheat or subcooling relies on:','["Duct length", "Thermostat color", "Filter size", "Pressure and temperature readings"]'::jsonb,3,'Measured values guide charge.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.A',3,'A step-down transformer produces a secondary voltage that is:','["Lower than the primary", "Higher than the primary", "Zero", "Equal always"]'::jsonb,0,'Step-down reduces voltage.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.A',4,'A common HVAC control transformer converts line voltage to:','["6 volts", "24 volts", "480 volts", "1,000 volts"]'::jsonb,1,'Control circuits use 24 V.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.B',3,'AC voltage is produced by a generator whose coil rotates in a:','["Oil sump", "Vacuum tube", "Magnetic field", "Water bath"]'::jsonb,2,'Rotation induces voltage.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.B',4,'One full cycle of an AC sine wave consists of a positive alternation and a:','["Zero alternation", "Double positive", "Square wave", "Negative alternation"]'::jsonb,3,'Sine waves alternate.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.C',3,'A 120/208 volt wye service from the utility feeds a commercial rooftop unit with how many hot conductors?','["3", "6", "1", "2"]'::jsonb,0,'Three phases.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.C',4,'Single-phase 240 volt service has two hot legs that are:','["120 degrees apart", "180 degrees apart", "90 degrees apart", "In phase"]'::jsonb,1,'Split-phase.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.D',3,'In an inductive circuit, the current:','["Leads the voltage", "Is in phase", "Lags the voltage", "Is zero"]'::jsonb,2,'Inductors lag.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.D',4,'In a capacitive circuit, the current:','["Is steady DC", "Lags the voltage", "Equals zero", "Leads the voltage"]'::jsonb,3,'Capacitors lead.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.E',3,'A run capacitor stays in the circuit to improve motor:','["Efficiency while running", "Fuel mix", "Humidity", "Duct size"]'::jsonb,0,'Run caps are continuous duty.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.E',4,'A start capacitor is in the circuit only:','["All the time", "For a brief time while the motor starts", "After shutdown", "Never"]'::jsonb,1,'Start caps are intermittent.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.F',3,'A three-phase induction motor does not need a start capacitor because the three phases create:','["A frozen field", "No field", "A rotating magnetic field", "A steady DC field"]'::jsonb,2,'Phases self-start.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.F',4,'Single-phase motors need a starting mechanism because a single-phase field:','["Is absent", "Rotates fast", "Is DC", "Pulses rather than rotates"]'::jsonb,3,'No starting torque without help.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.G',3,'A permanent split-capacitor motor is often used in:','["Fan applications", "Welding", "Arc lighting", "Large chillers"]'::jsonb,0,'PSC motors drive fans.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.G',4,'A shaded-pole motor is a low-torque motor often found in:','["Chillers", "Small fans and appliances", "Industrial pumps", "Elevators"]'::jsonb,1,'Simple, low-cost motor.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.H',3,'When testing a capacitor, always first:','["Cut it", "Heat it", "Discharge it safely", "Charge it more"]'::jsonb,2,'Stored charge is dangerous.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.H',4,'Capacitors can hold a shock hazard even when the power is:','["Disconnected from the earth", "On only", "At 5 volts", "Off"]'::jsonb,3,'Stored energy remains.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.I',3,'A capacitor is tested with a meter set to measure:','["Capacitance in microfarads", "Speed", "Humidity", "Frequency of gas"]'::jsonb,0,'Compare to rating.','original'),
('127.112.d.6.I',4,'A transformer winding that reads infinite resistance is:','["Grounded", "Open", "Shorted", "Good"]'::jsonb,1,'Open circuit.','original'),
('127.112.d.7.A',3,'Semiconductor materials such as silicon conduct:','["Exactly like rubber", "Better than copper", "Better than insulators but less than metals", "Only when frozen"]'::jsonb,2,'Semiconductors are in between.','original'),
('127.112.d.7.A',4,'Adding impurities to silicon to change its conductivity is called:','["Soldering", "Melting", "Plating", "Doping"]'::jsonb,3,'Doping creates N and P types.','original'),
('127.112.d.7.B',3,'A diode allows current to flow:','["In one direction", "Only at night", "In both directions", "In no direction"]'::jsonb,0,'Diodes are one-way valves.','original'),
('127.112.d.7.B',4,'An LED emits light when current passes:','["Across a gap", "Forward through it", "In reverse", "Never"]'::jsonb,1,'Forward bias lights it.','original'),
('127.112.d.7.C',3,'Resistors are marked with:','["Sound waves", "Magnets", "Color bands", "Letters of the alphabet only"]'::jsonb,2,'Bands give value.','original'),
('127.112.d.7.C',4,'A variable resistor used to adjust volume is a:','["Fuse", "Relay", "Diode", "Potentiometer"]'::jsonb,3,'Pots change resistance.','original'),
('127.112.d.7.D',3,'A thermistor changes resistance with:','["Temperature", "Voltage", "Sound", "Color"]'::jsonb,0,'Thermal sensor.','original'),
('127.112.d.7.D',4,'A cad cell changes resistance with:','["Heat", "Light", "Humidity", "Pressure"]'::jsonb,1,'Cad cells sense light.','original'),
('127.112.d.7.E',3,'A diode is tested with a meter on the:','["Amps setting live", "Frequency setting only", "Diode test or resistance setting", "Gas setting"]'::jsonb,2,'Check forward and reverse.','original'),
('127.112.d.7.E',4,'A good diode reads low resistance one way and:','["Infinite both ways", "Low both ways", "Zero both ways", "Very high resistance the other way"]'::jsonb,3,'One-way conduction.','original'),
